<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Two Ewalds</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 695</span> · Also known as Ewald the Black, Ewald the Fair</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The two Ewalds, called Ewald the Black and Ewald the Fair, were English priests who went as missionaries to the Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> around 695. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> they were killed by Saxons who feared the conversion of their chieftain, and their bodies were thrown into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. They are venerated as saints and martyrs, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, where their relics lie.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/two-ewalds.jpg" alt="Two Ewalds" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: user:itti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ewalde_scan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
